course: dictionary
lesson: 655
title: The many meanings of bag
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A bag is a soft container you carry things in, such as a school bag, a shopping bag or a lunch bag. The word can also mean a sleeping bag or a tea bag. Read the words around bag to find out which kind is meant.
explain: «A seat is for a person, so put your bag on your lap or the floor when the bus is busy.» Here bag is something you carry, and the clues are lap and floor. Try the swap: "put your container on your lap" still makes sense, so a bag is a kind of container.
explain: Bag can also be a verb. «I bagged the front seat» means I grabbed it for myself before anyone else. Casual talk does the same with «Bags the window seat!», which claims the seat first. Here bag does not mean putting anything in a sack.
explain: Some uses are idioms, so the words around them matter most. «Bags of time» means lots of time, and «in the bag» means sure to happen. Neither phrase is about a real bag, so do not look for one.
fact: A bag is a soft container you carry things in, made of paper, plastic, cloth or leather.
fact: «Bags» means you claim something first in the UK, Ireland and Australia; in the US people say «dibs».
fact: «Bags of» means lots of, as in «bags of time».
fact: Formal words for bag include «container», «receptacle», «sack» and «parcel».
fact: Never carry packages or bags for strangers.
exercise: «A seat is for a person, so put your bag on your lap or the floor when the bus is busy.» Where should the bag go? | In the road | *On your lap or the floor | Under the bus | On the seat
exercise: «I bagged the front seat.» What does bagged mean here? | Put it in a sack of potatoes | *Grabbed it for yourself first | Looked at it closely | Lost it on the bus
exercise: Three bags each hold 4 sweets. How many sweets are there in total? | *12 | 34 | 1 | 7
test: Sam shouts, «Bags the window seat!» What is he doing? | *Claiming the window seat before anyone else | Walking slowly to the window | Getting cross with his friends | Putting his bag on the seat
test: «Bags of time» means what? | A small amount of time | *Two bags with time inside | Lots of time | Time that is lost
exam: In a formal story, which word could replace bag in «She filled her ___ with books»? | *Container | Trainer | Sweet | Bus
